# A Semitransparent Inorganic Perovskite Film for Overcoming Ultraviolet Light Instability of Organic Solar Cells and Achieving 14.03% Efficiency

## abstract
Organic solar cells (OSCs) can be unstable under ultraviolet (UV) irradiation. To address this issue and enhance the power conversion efficiency (PCE), an inorganic-perovskite/organic four-terminal tandem solar cell (TSC) based on a semitransparent inorganic CsPbBr3 perovskite solar cell (pero-SC) as the top cell and an OSC as bottom cell is constructed. The high-quality CsPbBr3 photoactive layer of the planar pero-SC is prepared with a dual-source vacuum coevaporation method, using stoichiometric precursors of CsBr and PbBr2 with a low evaporation rate. The resultant opaque planar pero-SC exhibits an ultrahigh open-circuit voltage of 1.44 V and the highest reported PCE of 7.78% for a CsPbBr3 -based planar pero-SC. Importantly, the devices show no degradation after 120 h UV light illumination. The related semitransparent pero-SC can almost completely filter UV light and well maintain photovoltaic performance; it additionally shows an extremely high average visible transmittance. When it is used to construct a TSC, the top pero-SC acting as a UV filter can utilize UV light for photoelectric conversion, avoiding the instability problem of UV light on the bottom OSC that can meet the industrial standards of UV-light stability for solar cells, and leading to the highest reported PCE of 14.03% for the inorganic-perovskite/organic TSC.

Organic solar cells (OSCs) can be unstable under ultraviolet (UV) irradiation. To address this issue and enhance the power conversion efficiency (PCE), an inorganic-perovskite/organic four-terminal tandem solar cell (TSC) based on a semitransparent inorganic $\mathsf{c s P b}\mathsf{B r}_{3}$ perovskite solar cell (pero-SC) as the top cell and an OSC as bottom cell is constructed. The high-quality $\mathsf{c s P b}\mathsf{B r}_{3}$ photoactive layer of the planar pero-SC is prepared with a dual-source vacuum coevaporation method, using stoichiometric precursors of CsBr and $\mathsf{P b B}\mathsf{r}_{2}$ with a low evaporation rate. The resultant opaque planar pero-SC exhibits an ultrahigh open-circuit voltage of 1.44 V and the highest reported PCE of $7.78\%$ for a $\mathsf{c s P b B r}_{3}$ -based planar pero-SC. Importantly, the devices show no degradation after $\mathtt{l20h}$ UV light illumination. The related semitransparent pero-SC can almost completely filter UV light and well maintain photo voltaic performance; it additionally shows an extremely high average visible transmittance. When it is used to construct a TSC, the top pero-SC acting as a UV filter can utilize UV light for photoelectric conversion, avoiding the instability problem of UV light on the bottom OSC that can meet the industrial standards of UV-light stability for solar cells, and leading to the highest reported PCE of $14.03\%$ for the inorganic-perovskite/organic TSC.
In recent years, perovskite solar cells (pero-SCs) and organic solar cells (OSCs) have attracted tremendous attention because of their low cost, semitransparency, flexibility, and the rapid growth of their power conversion efficiencies (PCEs).[1–5] The certified record PCEs of single-junction pero-SCs and OSCs have reached $22.1\%^{[6]}$ and $13\%,\%$ respectively. However, the stability of such solar cells is still unsatisfactory and is the main bottleneck that prevents their commercialization.[8,9] The organic–inorganic hybrid halide perovskite materials mainly suffer from inferior stabilities against heat, moisture, and light, whereas the organic materials are vulnerable to oxidation and photodegradation under exposure to ultraviolet (UV) light. Therefore, exploring photovoltaic materials with high stability and designing new device structures are imperative for promoting the future commercial applications of these materials.
Recently, inorganic halide perovskites in which the organic cation has been replaced with an inorganic cation, $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ $\mathrm{(X=Cl}$ , Br, I, or their mixture), have been garnering intensive attention for use in optoelectronic devices because of their outstanding optical properties and high stability.[10–13] Among these materials, $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ has demonstrated the most promising stability against heat and humidity.[14–16] Jin and co-workers[14] and Tang et al.[17] reported that $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based mesoporous pero-SCs displayed no performance degradation when exposed to highly humid air and extreme temperatures for more than 3 months. Unlike the organic–inorganic hybrid perovskites, $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ has not been shown to be a very efficient solar material because of its broad bandgap of ${\approx}2.3\ \mathrm{\eV}$ leading to a relatively low short-circuit current $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ ;[18] however, $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ appears to efficiently harvest short-wavelength sunlight in the wavelength range from 300 to $530~\mathrm{nm}$ . The highest reported external quantum efficiency (EQE) of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based pero-SCs has exceeded $\approx90\%$ at ${\approx}400~\mathrm{nm}$ .[18] Importantly, the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based pero-SCs generally show a high open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of ${\approx}1.25\ \mathrm{V}$ together with a high transmittance in the visible region at wavelengths longer than $530\mathrm{nm}$ . Thus, the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based pero-SCs are of significant interest for possible use in semitransparent and tandem solar cells (TSCs). However, solution-processed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}$ films still show a slight photodegradation under UV irradiation.[15] The imperfect photostability of the solution-processed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ likely originates from the solution process, which can easily introduce high concentration of defects, leading to a low tolerance toward UV light.[19]
The bandgaps of the photoactive layer materials of OSCs can be finely tuned to less than $1.5\ \mathrm{eV},$ enabling the harvesting of long-wavelength solar light, leading to a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ exceeding ${\approx}20\ \mathrm{\mA\}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ .[2,7,20,21] However, the stability of the photovoltaic materials under UV-light irradiation could be problematic for the commercialization of OSCs.[22–24] Although this issue can be addressed by attaching a UV-cut filter on the front of glass substrates for blocking most UV photons, the $\approx20\%$ loss of the total solar light energy mainly in the range from 300 to $550\mathrm{nm}$ would undoubtedly lead to a sacrifice of the device performance.[25] Therefore, TSCs with the UV-absorbing $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}.$ based pero-SC as the top cell and the visible–near-infraredabsorbing OSC as the bottom cell could represent an ideal combination from the viewpoints of complementary absorption in the UV–vis–NIR region and stability against UV light. This strategy can simultaneously filter UV light without sacrificing OSC performance. Nevertheless, several major challenges need to be addressed before realizing this technology in real use, including i) obtaining a high-quality defect-free $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film with high stability under UV-light irradiation; ii) fabricating a high-transparency $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based top pero-SC, and iii) optimizing the complementary absorption between the top and bottom solar cells.
In this work, we developed a high-quality $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ inorganic perovskite using the dual-source vacuum coevaporation (DSVE) method. We obtained high-quality $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}$ films with a low defect concentration and negligible grain boundaries using stoichiometric precursors and a low evaporation rate. A planar pero-SC fabricated using the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film as the photo­ active layer exhibited an ultrahigh $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.44~\mathrm{V},$ the highest PCE of $7.78\%$ reported to date for a $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based planar peroSC, and outstanding UV-light stability. The related semitransparent pero-SC not only exhibited promising average visible transmittance (AVT) of $\approx60\%$ in the visible region longer than $530~\mathrm{nm}$ , but also maintained good stability and ultrahigh $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ which is a favorable candidate as the top cell for the fabrication of four-terminal TSCs with an OSC as the bottom cell. The top $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based cell can simultaneously utilize the UV light for the photo­electric conversion and filter the UV light that is harmful to the stability of the OSCs. The resulting TSCs with the OSC based on the wide-bandgap polymer PBDB-T-SF as the donor and a narrow-bandgap n-type organic semiconductor IT-4F as the acceptor exhibited a promising PCE of $14.03\%$ and excellent photostability, retaining more than $99.4\%$ of their initial PCEs after $120\mathrm{h}$ of UV-light illumination, which is the best photovoltaic performance reported for an inorganic-perovskite/ organic TSC. This strategy can well address the bottleneck of photostability for OSCs without efficiency loss.
In the fabrication of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based pero-SCs, the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ photoactive layer is commonly prepared using a two-step sequential solution process.[26] However, fabricating smooth, pinhole-free $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ perovskite layers on planar substrates is difficult because of incomplete reaction between the CsBr and $\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{2}$ bilayers. Here, we prepared $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ perovskite layers by the DSVE method, as shown in Figure 1a. In the vacuum evaporation step, we simultaneously evaporated two metal bromide precursors, CsBr and $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ , onto a $\mathsf{Z n O}/$ ITO glass substrate, effectively avoiding the “vapor-gas-like” behavior and other problems associated with the distinct properties of organic halides.[27] By controlling the molar ratios and evaporation rates, the coevaporation process ensured the sufficient mixing and complete reaction of the two metal bromides. The mixed film was then thermally annealed at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $15~\mathrm{min}$ under a $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, which can accelerate and complete the crystal phase transition to a single-phase perovskite crystal film.[28] The resulting $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ thin film shows semitransparent characteristics, with a high average visible transmittance of $90.9\%$ in the wavelength range from 530 to ${800}\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1b).[14,29,30] Notably, even when the two metal bromides were evaporated at different molar ratios $\mathrm{(PbBr_{2}/C s B r=1:1.2}$ 1:1, and 1:0.8), the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ films still exhibited similar absorption spectra but different absorption intensities (Figure 1c). These results indicate that DSVE is an effective method for fabricating cubic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ polycrystals.
We measured the phase purity and characterized the crystal structure of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ films by X-ray diffraction (XRD) (Figure 1d). As expected, the XRD patterns of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ films prepared using various precursor ratios matched well with the cubic phase $(P m3m)$ .[31] In particular, the sample prepared using a stoichiometric mixture of precursors $(\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{CsBr}=1{:}1$ ) not only showed the highest diffraction peak intensity but also presented high purity, which indicates its high crystallinity. By contrast, when $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ was rich $\mathrm{(PbBr_{2}/C s B r=}$ 1:0.8), some additional peaks at $11.6^{\circ}$ and $23.4^{\circ}$ (assigned to the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}\mathrm{Br}_{5}$ phase) appeared. The existence of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{2}\mathrm{Br}_{5}$ phase can enhance electron–hole recombination, thus leading to a decreased $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ for the solar cells.[32] For the CsBr-rich deposition $\mathrm{(PbBr_{2}/C s B r=1:1.2)}$ , the excess CsBr indicated by the characteristic peak at $29.4^{\circ}$ would easily lead to a high defect concentration in the crystalline $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ .[19] Therefore, we conclude that the 1:1 ratio of the two precursors is the best choice for preparing crystalline $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ ; the use of an excess of either precursor is detrimental to the growth of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ crystal structure and would likely lead to low crystallization and the formation of a high density of trap states. The consistent $\mathrm{PbBr}_{2}/\mathrm{CsBr}$ ratios were determined by elemental analysis via X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy measurement (Figure S1 and Table S1, Supporting Information).
The effect of the evaporation rates on the growth of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ perovskite film was investigated using stoichiometric precursors (molar ratio of $\mathrm{PbBr}_{2}\mathrm{:CsBr}$ is 1:1) at rates of 5, 15, and $25\textup{\AA s}^{-1}$ . As shown in Figure 2a, the evaporation rates did not alter the cubic phase of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ but strongly influenced their crystallinity and crystal orientation. We found that a lower evaporation rate led to higher crystallinity and to (100, 200) crystal orientation. The $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film obtained by evaporation at the lowest rate of $5\mathrm{~\AA~}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ showed the highest crystallinity along the (100, 200) crystal orientation, which would undoubtedly affect the morphology of the perovskite film. As expected, the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film evaporated at $5\mathrm{~\AA~}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ exhibited a desirable morphology with compact and large grains (Figure 2b), which is favorable for a high-performance device because the compact and large grain size perovskite crystals reduce the area of the grain boundaries in which most defects are located. By contrast, when films were prepared with evaporation at a high rate (15 or $25\textup{\AA s}^{-1})$ , the grain sizes were dramatically reduced; this effect was accompanied by an inhomogeneous grain distribution. Based on the Volmer–Weber growth mode for vacuum thermal evaporation,[33] we propose a mechanism for the effect of the evaporation rates on the growth of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film as shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information). At low evaporation rates, $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ :CsBr molecules possess a low kinetic energy and can more easily attach to the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface of the substrate electrode. With the continuous capture of the incident $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ :CsBr molecules, these molecules with low kinetic energy facilitate the formation of stable and homogeneous nuclei, which then stack discretely on the substrate in the island-like structure. Subsequently, the island-like structures coalesce and undergo crystal growth along their preferred crystal orientation (100, 200) with the assistance of the low-kinetic-energy $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ :CsBr molecules. Finally, a uniform $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film with large grain size and high orientation is formed. By contrast, the high evaporation rate makes the attachment of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ :CsBr molecules onto the substrates difficult; because of their high kinetic energy, the subsequent coalescence inevitably leads to $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ films with cracks and defects accompanied by small grains and a low degree of crystallization.
In addition to the crystallization and morphology, the nature of the defects in the perovskite films is another critical factor that affects the photogenerated carrier behavior and device performance. We investigated the trap states of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ films by measuring their steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL). Notably, the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ films used for the PL measurements were evaporated onto the bare glass substrate and the average decay time $(\tau_{\mathrm{ave}})$ of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ films were calculated according to the formula $\tau_{\alpha}=$ ${(A_{1}{\tau_{1}}^{2}+A_{2}{\tau_{2}}^{2})}/{(A_{1}\tau_{1}+A_{2}\tau_{2})}$ .[34] The obtained results are shown in Figure 2c and are summarized in Table S2 (Supporting Information). The PL lifetimes of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ films were substantially prolonged with the decrease in the evaporation rate from 25 to $5\mathring{\textrm{A}}\mathring{\mathbf{s}}^{-1}$ ; this result is attributed to the reduced carrier recombination in the bulk $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ . A similar result was deduced from the steady-state PL spectra, which showed the enhanced PL intensity for the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film prepared with a lower deposition rate. Taking the high-purity $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ phase into account, we tentatively attributed the reduced carrier recombination to the fewer trap states originating from the negligible grain boundaries and large grain size.[35]
To determine the exact band structures of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film fabricated by the DSVE method, we characterized it by ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy. As shown in Figure $\mathtt{S3a\mathrm{-c}}$ (Supporting Information), the work function $(\boldsymbol{W}_{\mathrm{S}})$ , valance band maximum (VBM), and bandgap $(E_{\mathrm{g}})$ of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film are 4.57, 1.29, and $2.36\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. The valence band $(E_{\mathrm{V}})$ and conduction band $(E_{\mathrm{C}})$ of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ semiconductor film are $-5.86$ and $-3.50\ \mathrm{eV}$ respectively, as obtained via the equations $E_{\mathrm{V}}=-(\operatorname{W}_{\mathrm{S}}+\mathrm{VBM})$ and $E_{\mathrm{C}}=E_{\mathrm{V}}+E_{\mathrm{g}}$ .[36] These values are slightly downshifted compared with those of the cubic lattice of the solution-processed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}$ film $(E_{\mathrm{C}}\approx-3.3\mathrm{eV},$ $E_{\mathrm{V}}\approx-5.6\ \mathrm{eV},$ ),[14,37] likely because of their distinct crystal orientation. In addition, as shown in the energy-level diagram (Figure S3d, Supporting Information), the downward $E_{\mathrm{C}}$ of the vacuum-evaporated $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film is closer to those of the conventionally used electron transport layers (ETLs) such as $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ $(-4.0\ \mathrm{eV})^{[38]}$ and ZnO (−4.2 eV),[39] which could enhance the charge transfer from the perovskite to the electron transport layer.[36] It should be mentioned that we used $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ as ETL in fabricating pero-SCs in this work and did not mention the widely used $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL because there is UV photocatalytic effect of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ which could cause degradation of perovskite and limit its application in the devices.[40]
To study the photovoltaic behavior of the pero-SCs based on the vacuum-evaporated $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ films, devices with the configuration of $\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}/\$ spiro-OMeTAD/Au were fabricated. In the cross-section SEM image of the optimized device (Figure 3a), all functional layers are clearly distinguished. The grain sizes are obviously larger than the film thickness, and the grains could be uniformly expanded to several micrometers along the substrate. Importantly, together with compact and homogeneous grains in the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film, the grain boundaries are negligible and perpendicular to the substrate, effectively minimizing the grain-boundary energy and trap states, as demonstrated previously.[41] The $J{-}V$ characteristics of the pero-SCs with the optimized $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film (stoichiometric precursors, evaporation rate of $5\mathrm{~\AA~s^{-1}~}$ , thickness of $450~\mathrm{nm}$ ) were measured under simulated solar illumination of AM 1.5G $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Figure S4 in the Supporting Information shows the effect of the active layer thickness on the current density and PCE values of the pero-SCs, and the optimized thickness is $450~\mathrm{nm}$ . As shown in Figure 3b, the best device among the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based pero-SCs achieved a PCE of $7.78\%$ with a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $7.01\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.44~\mathrm{V},$ and a fill factor (FF) of $77.11\%$ . The $7.78\%$ efficiency is much higher than the values reported for solution-processed $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}$ -based devices[14,15] and is also the highest value reported for an $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based planar peroSC. Notably, an ultrahigh $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.44\mathrm{~V~}$ was obtained, which is obviously higher than that of the traditional $\mathsf{M A P b I}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ , and mixed-perovskite-based pero-SCs because of the relatively wide bandgap of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ $(\approx2.3\ \mathrm{eV})$ .[42,43] Even compared with the device fabricated with solution-processed cubic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ $\scriptstyle(\approx1.26\lor)$ ,[14,15,18] the vacuum-evaporated $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based device also showed an improved $V_{\mathrm{oc}}.$ This result is most likely attributable to the low density of trap states in the vacuum-evaporated $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film as a result of its high crystallinity, large grain size, and negligible grain boundaries. A similar result was reported by Huang and co-workers, who revealed that surface charge traps in the perovskite film caused the majority of the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ losses in pero-SCs.[44] Nevertheless, a slight hysteresis behavior was still observed between the forward and reverse scan modes, as illustrated in Figure S5 and Table S3 (Supporting Information); the average PCE of $7.51\%$ for the forward scan is comparable to the PCE of $7.78\%$ in the reverse scan. To check the reproducibility of the devices, a histogram of the $J{-}V$ scan efficiencies for 25 independent cells was constructed, showing a narrow distribution with the average value of $6.95\%$ (see the inset of Figure 3b). The excellent device performance and its high reproducibility are attributed to the high-quality $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film, which again demonstrates that DSVE is a promising method for preparing the inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ perovskite films. Figure 3c shows the external quantum efficiency spectrum of the bestperforming device. The $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ value calculated by integration of the EQE spectrum is $7.31\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ , in good agreement (slightly higher) with the values obtained from the $J{-}V$ curves. The high EQE of $93.3\%$ at $400~\mathrm{nm}$ indicates good utilization of photons in the short-wavelength $(300-530~\mathrm{nm}$ ) region of sunlight, particularly in the UV region.
Considering the high transmittance of intrinsic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film in the visible region, we further fabricated the related semitransparent pero-SC using transfer-laminated transparent PH1000 (highly conductive PEDOT:PSS) electrode to replace the opaque Au electrode. The PH1000 electrode showed a good energy-level alignment with the hole-transporting layer spiro-OMeTAD and an AVT as high as $80\%$ in the $530{-}800~\mathrm{nm}$ range (Figure S6a, Supporting Information).[45] As shown in
Figure 3d, the architectural background can be seen clearly through the resulting semitransparent device, accompanied by an extremely high AVT (longer than $530~\mathrm{nm}$ ) of $59.8\%$ and completely filtered UV light (Figure S6b, Supporting Information). Importantly, the semitransparent $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based pero-SC could also well maintain photovoltaic performance, exhibiting a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $6.15\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.38~\mathrm{V},$ an FF of $70.51\%$ , and a PCE of $5.98\%$ when measured in the reverse scan direction (Figure 3e and Table 1). The $\approx10\%$ loss of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ compared with that of the corresponding opaque device indicates that most light in the $300{-}530\ \mathrm{nm}$ wavelength range was absorbed by the top $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ . The slight decrease in the FF is attributable to the reduced Ohmic contact of the semitransparent device, as evidenced by the slightly increased series resistances from $11.13\Omega\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ for the opaque device to $12.87\Omega\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ for the semitransparent device. A histogram of the PCE distribution for 25 independent semitransparent pero-SCs was shown in the inset of Figure 3e, indicating a high reproducibility.
Given the unfavorable light-soaking stability of the solutionprocessed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}[15]$ and the high photoresponse of the vacuum-evaporated $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based device at short wavelengths, we investigated the UV-light stability of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based devices. Devices with a vacuum-evaporated $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ film were continuously exposed to UV light ( $365\ \mathrm{nm}$ wavelength) under a $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere and a UV-light intensity of $100\ensuremath{\mathrm{~mW~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-2}$ , which is almost equivalent to that of 22 suns and $120\ensuremath{~\mathrm{kWh}\mathrm{m}^{-2}}$ of UV-light radiation power.[40,46] According to the IEC-61345 industrial standard, the solar cells could meet the industrial requirement in UV stability when it maintains over $95\%$ of its initial efficiency after receiving $15\ensuremath{~\mathrm{kWh}~\mathrm{m}^{-2}}$ of UV irradiation power.[47] Therefore, as shown in Figure 3f, the almost no degradation pero-SCs efficiencies after aging $120\mathrm{h}$ demonstrated the high photostability of the vacuum-evaporated $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based device far beyond industrial standard. The good photostability is most likely attributable to the low density of trap states in the vacuum-evaporated bulk $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ because the trap sites are the most likely sites for the onset of degradation.[46] Therefore, the high photostability of the vacuum-evaporated $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based device opens a new route for its application.
The semitransparent pero-SCs based on the vacuum-evaporated $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ showed high transmittance, completely filtered UV light and good photovoltaic performance, in particular for their excellent photostability in the UV-light region. Therefore, we fabricated four-terminal TSCs with the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based peroSC as the top cell and an OSC as the bottom cell (Figure 4a) to overcome the UV-light stability issue of the OSCs and for fully utilizing sunlight.[48] To demonstrate the universality of above strategy, we selected three organic photoactive layer materials with various bandgaps and stabilities, including poly[4,8-bis(5-(2-ethylhexyl)thiophen-2-yl)benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b′] dithiophene-co-3-fluorothieno[3,4-b]thiophene-2-carboxylate][49] (PTB7-Th):[6,6]- phenyl- $\cdot\mathrm{C}_{71}$ -butyric acid methyl ester[50] $(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM})$ , poly[(2,6-(4,8-bis(5-(2-ethylhexyl)thiophen-2-yl)- benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b′]dithiophene))-alt-(5,5-( $^{1^{\prime},3^{\prime}}$ -di-2-thienyl- $5^{\prime},7^{\prime}$ - bis(2-ethylhexyl)benzo[ $1^{\prime},2^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{c}{:}4^{\prime},5^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{c}$ ′]dithiophene-4,8-dione))] (PBDB-T):3,9-bis(2-methylene-(3-(1,1-dicyanomethylene)-indanone))-5,5,11,11-tetrakis(4-hexylphenyl)-dithieno $[2,3\mathrm{-d};2^{\prime},3^{\prime}$ - $\mathrm{d^{\prime}}]$ -s-indaceno[1,2-b:5,6-b′]dithiophene[51] (ITIC), and PBDB-TSF:IT-4F;[7] their molecular structures are shown in Figure 4a. The organic photoactive layers show broad absorption even extending to the NIR region, which can well complement the absorption of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ pero-SCs in the long-wavelength region. In the short-wavelength range of $300{-}530~\mathrm{nm}$ , there was somewhat overlap EQE region for the opaque $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ pero-SCs and the OSCs (Figure S7, Supporting Information).[20] As shown in Figure 4b, the differences of the integrated $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values from the EQE spectra (OSC versus pero-SC) in the wavelength range $300{-}530\ \mathrm{nm}$ are $-1.1$ , $-1.6$ , and $-1.7\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for the OSCs based on PTB7-Th: $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ , PBDB-T:ITIC, and PBDB-T-SF:IT4F, respectively. These results indicate that the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based pero-SC could more efficiently utilize the short wavelength of solar spectrum compared with the OSCs. The sunlight utilization spectra and rate in the UV and the short-wavelength range $(<530\ \mathrm{nm})$ for each solar cell (Figure S8 and Table S4, Supporting information) further confirmed this conclusion. Therefore, the inorganic-perovskite/organic TSCs are expected to not only improve the photovoltaic performance but also overcome the UV-light stability problem of the OSCs, with the pero-SC acting as a UV filter.
The device structure of the resulting TSCs is illuminated in the top cell in Figure $4\mathrm{a}$ . The $J{-}V$ curves and EQE spectra of the semitransparent pero-SCs and the filtered bottom OSCs are shown in Figure 4c,d and Figure S9 (Supporting Information); the corresponding photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table 1. After the light is filtered by the top $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ - based semitransparent cell, the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of the bottom OSCs is expected to decrease because of the reduced light intensity and the FF is expected to increase because of the reduced effect of the series resistance at lower illumination. The resultant PTB7-Th: $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ and PBDB-T:ITIC-based four-terminal TSC exhibited an obviously increased PCEs of $10.61\%$ and $11.57\%$ , respectively, compared with those of the OSCs without the stacking pero-SC. The integrated $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values from the measured EQE (Figure S9b,d, Supporting Information) of the filtered bottom cell are also well consistent to those obtained from the corresponding $J{-}V$ curves (Figure S9a,c, Supporting Information). However, we note that the EQE values of both the bottom OSCs based on PTB7-Th: $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ and PBDB-T:ITIC are obviously lower compared with that of the top $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ cell. To further enhance the photocurrent contribution from the bottom OSCs, we used a blend of PBDB-T-SF:IT-4F as the photoactive layer in the bottom OSC for its broader absorption range (extended to the NIR region) and higher EQE values (in the long-wavelength range).[7] As expected, the filtered OSC based on PBDB-T-SF:IT-4F showed the EQE that was comparable to that of the top $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ cell (Figure 4d), which was further confirmed by the obviously improved $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ $(12.18~\mathrm{mA~cm^{-2}})$ obtained from the $J{-}V$ curve, leading to a PCE of $8.05\%$ (Figure 4c). The resulting four-terminal TSCs exhibited a PCE of $14.03\%$ (the highest value among ten individual devices), representing the first report of high-performance inorganic-perovskite/organic TSC.
The long-term stability and UV-light endurance of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}/$ organic four-terminal TSCs were subsequently evaluated. The unsealed TSCs and control OSCs with the structure of ITO/ZnO/photoactive layer $/\mathrm{MoO}_{3}/\mathrm{Al}$ were stored in a $\mathrm{N}_{2}.$ filled glovebox, and time-dependent PCE retentions (average values from ten devices) were measured at room temperature under UV-light irradiation ( $365~\mathrm{nm}$ , $100\mathrm{~mW~cm^{-2}},$ . As shown in Figure 5a, the control OSCs exhibited various degrees of degradation after continuous UV-light irradiation for $^{120\mathrm{~h~}}$ The fullerene OSCs (PTB7-Th: $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}_{.}^{\cdot}$ showed the most rapid degradation, retaining only $\approx46\%$ of their initial PCE after $120\mathrm{~h~}$ of UV irradiation. The dramatically degraded PCE is most likely attributable to the electrostatic potential energy of the fullerene derivatives triggered by the UV light and leading to the destruction of the nanomorphological order.[52] By contrast, the nonfullerene OSCs showed improved photostabilities, retaining $71\%$ and $93\%$ of their initial PCEs after $120\mathrm{~h~}$ of UV irradiation for the devices based on PBDB-T:ITIC and PBDB-T-SF:IT-4F, respectively. However, device performance was still obviously degraded, limiting their practical application. Encouragingly, as shown in Figure 5b, the photostability of the OSCs was dramatically improved when they were used as the bottom cell of the inorganic-perovskite/organic four-terminal TSCs. This improvement resulted from the top $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based pero-SC completely filtering the UV light, thereby effectively avoiding the UV illumination of the bottom OSCs. The TSCs with the nonfullerene OSCs based on PBDBT:ITIC and PBDB-T-SF:IT-4F retained more than $98\%$ of their initial PCEs, accompanied by well-maintained $J_{\mathrm{sc}},$ $V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ and FF values (Figure S10, Supporting Information), after aging $120\mathrm{~h~}$ under $100\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ UV light. Even for the PTB7-Th: $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ - based TSC, only a $10\%$ loss of the initial PCE was observed; the slight decrease in device performance could be caused by the elevated temperature due to the long-term UV-light illumination. All the aforementioned device photostability results are superior to the results reported in the literature for OSCs exposed under UV light.[25,52,53] Thus, as shown in Figure 5c, the three four-terminal TSCs containing various organic photoactive layer materials exhibited robust photostability, retaining $97.3\mathrm{-}99.4\%$ of their initial PCEs after strong UV-light irradiation at $100\mathrm{\mw\cm^{-2}}$ for $120\mathrm{~h~}$ . Moreover, these results confirmed that the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ -based semitransparent pero-SC is an ideal top cell for the four-terminal TSCs with the OSC as the bottom cell. Because the top semitransparent pero-SC could not only utilize the UV light for photoelectric conversion, but also avoid the UV-light instability of the OSCs.
We further investigated the degradation dynamics of the OSCs to better understand the enhanced photostability of the TSCs. Here, we take PBDB-T:ITIC-based OSCs as an example. To comprehensively study the influence of UV-light illumination on the excitation dissociation and charge collection properties of the OSCs, the plots of the photocurrent $(J_{\mathrm{ph}})$ versus the effective applied voltage $(V_{\mathrm{eff}})$ of two types of OSCs were measured (Figure 5d). One was the control OSC without the stacking on the pero-SC and the other is the bottom OSC stacked on the pero-SC in the TSC. After $120\mathrm{~h~}$ of UV-light aging, the $J_{\mathrm{ph}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}}$ of the control OSC was calculated to be $80.9\%$ . This result suggested that the UV light could degrade the devices, thus leading to a low exciton dissociation efficiency at the donor/acceptor interfaces and ineffective charge transport in the photoactive layer. By contrast, the filtered bottom OSC in the TSCs gives the high $J_{\mathrm{ph}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}}$ of $99.6\%$ , demonstrating that the top $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}.$ based pero-SC can efficiently suppress the effect of UV light on the bottom OSCs. This good stability was also confirmed by the well-maintained photoactive layer morphology (homogeneous and nanoscale phase separation) of the filtered bottom OSCs, whereas the control OSC showed obvious aggregation after aging (Figure S11, Supporting Information), which could lead to unfavorable charge separation and transport.[54]
We further investigated the loss mechanism of the photostability under the device operating conditions. Under shortcircuit conditions, the photogenerated electrons and holes are efficiently swept out of the device prior to recombination. As shown by the fitting of the relationship of the photocurrent and light intensity $(P_{\mathrm{light}})$ (Figure 5e),[55] the power-law exponents $(\alpha)$ of the equation $J_{\mathrm{ph}}\propto\ P_{\mathrm{light}}{}^{\alpha}$ for the control OSCs and the filtered bottom OSCs in the TSC are 0.98 and 1.00, respectively. An $\alpha$ value closer to unity suggests that UV-light irradiation only slightly affects the bimolecular recombination of the OSC and that the filtered OSC can entirely avoid the influence of the UV light on bimolecular recombination. Under open-circuit conditions, all photogenerated free carriers recombine. The recombination can be described by $V_{\mathrm{oc}}\propto(n k T/q)\mathrm{ln}(P)$ (Figure 5f),[56] where $k,\ T,$ and $\boldsymbol{q}$ are the Boltzmann constant, the absolute temperature, and the elementary charge, respectively. The fitted lines show the slope $(n)$ of 1.62 for the control OSC and 1.08 for the filtered bottom OSCs in the TSC. The obviously greater slope value of the control device suggests that the mono­molecular recombination is the dominant factor influencing the photostability because of the increased trap density in the photo­active layer after long-term UV-light irradiation. These results indicate that the use of a $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}$ -based semitransparent device as the top cell of the four-terminal TSC can effectively prevent the organic photoactive layer from being damaged by UV light.
In summary, we developed a new concept to address the bottleneck of photostability for OSCs without efficiency loss by constructing a $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}$ /organic four-terminal TSC. We first prepared high-quality $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ inorganic perovskite films with a low defect concentration using the dual-source vacuum thermal evaporation method. Compared to the pero-SCs based on solution-processed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ , the planar pero-SC based on the vacuum-evaporated $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}$ exhibits an ultrahigh $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.44\mathrm{V}$ and the highest PCE of $7.78\%$ reported so far for the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ - based planar pero-SCs as well as excellent photostability against UV-light irradiation. The semitransparent pero-SC based on this $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ with the transfer-laminated PH1000 as the transparent anode shows a PCE of $5.98\%$ with a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $6.15\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.38~\mathrm{V},$ and an FF of $70.51\%$ . The four-terminal TSCs were fabricated with the semitransparent pero-SC as the top cell stacked with OSC as the bottom cell to exploit the advantages of the pero-SC. The pero-SC will harvest UV light for photoelectric conversion and filter the UV light for overcoming the stability problem of the OSCs. The resulting four-terminal TSCs with a bottom OSC based on PBDB-T-SF:IT-4F exhibited the best PCE of $14.03\%$ and excellent photostability retention after $120\mathrm{{h}}$ of strong UV irradiation at $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The PCE of $14.03\%$ is the highest efficiency reported for an inorganicperovskite/organic TSC. This facile strategy can address the UV-light stability issue of OSCs and substantially improve the PCE, thus opening a new avenue for the further development and practical application of stable and low-cost OSCs.
